in haiti it appears death is never too  far away  saturday's devastating earthquake has  pushed the demand for coffins  and if that weren't enough haitians are  now preparing for another potential  disaster  tropical storm grace is set to make  landfall  bringing with it the risk of flash  floods landslides and further  complicating efforts to find survivors  we are bracing for tropical storm grace  that is coming so we have to have a  contingency so we can address the people  that are  that out of fear stay out of their  houses and sleep out on the streets  the magnitude 7.2 earthquake has added  to the list of crises haitians had been  dealing with  from gang violence that has affected  every aspect of life for many to last  month's assassination of president  jovenel muis and of course the covet 19  pandemic  here in colombia an all-out effort is  underway as regional nations send  desperately needed aid  the scale and damage becomes clear from  above  but this is the scene on the ground  stretcher beds and mattresses sprawled  among the rubble under the shade of  trees  the lack of medical resources is clear  with thousands in need of care doctors  are left to treat patients outside  hospital wards are overwhelmed with  health care workers struggling to cope  haiti's prime minister flew to the  seafront town of likai in the nation's  southwest an area which had been worst  affected by the quake  when i arrived in the south of the  country we saw the destruction many  houses are destroyed rescue teams are  working to find victims and survivors in  the rubble one thing i noticed was the  dignity of the people despite the  situation they are affected but  resilient they fight to survive  [Music]  on the streets of lakhai locals line up  to feed their families but this is far  from business as usual  football fields sprawled with survivors  forced to shelter with the few  belongings they have left  since the earthquake i didn't feel well  we're on the football field our home is  destroyed we can't drink the water and  many people are staying here  many here are still traumatized by the  2010 earthquake that killed hundreds of  thousands of people near the capital  port-au-prince  for many the only safe place to sleep  for now is on the streets  but the looming tropical storm brings  with it more risks leaving haitians  feeling hopeless with no place to go  wondering when things will get better  jillian wolf al jazeera
 

